## 2. Why the Two Defects Occur

Let

\[
L=\mathcal O_E(3p_0),\qquad \deg L=3,
\]

and let `a` be the selected section of
\(A_H=\nu^*\mathcal O(H)\) on the elliptic normalization \(E_B\). The frozen
Hartshorne-Serre map on the \(j\)-th qutrit chart uses the theta triple

\[
(aT_j,T_{j+1},T_{j+2}).
\]

Relative to the theta basis, its determinant is \(\pm a\). Since
\(\deg A_H=2\), every nonzero ordinary determinant section in this frozen
bundle type has a zero divisor of degree two. The selected `a` has two
simple zeros, so the two exceptional fibers are forced by the ordinary
determinant line. Replacing `a` by another scalar section cannot remove
them.

At either zero, the B-field-corrected rank-two part is

\[
N\oplus P,\qquad
\deg N=3,\qquad
P=L^{-1}\otimes N,\quad\deg P=0.
\]

The summand \(P\) is the source of the shifted skyscraper.

## 3. Exact Stable Recombination

Use the evaluation sequence

\[
0\longrightarrow Q^\vee\longrightarrow
\mathcal O_E^{\oplus3}
\xrightarrow{(T_0,T_1,T_2)}L\longrightarrow0.
\]

Inside \(H^0(Q^\vee\otimes L)\), define the three Koszul syzygies

\[
\begin{aligned}
s_0&=(0,T_2,-T_1),\\
s_1&=(-T_2,0,T_0),\\
s_2&=(T_1,-T_0,0).
\end{aligned}
\]

Each satisfies \(T_0s_{j,0}+T_1s_{j,1}+T_2s_{j,2}=0\). Each is nowhere
zero on the Fermat cubic: vanishing would force two homogeneous coordinates
to vanish, and no coordinate point lies on
\(T_0^3+T_1^3+T_2^3=0\).

A syzygy therefore gives a subbundle injection. The determinant identifies
the quotient and yields

\[
0\longrightarrow\mathcal O_E
\xrightarrow{s_j}Q^\vee\otimes L
\longrightarrow L\longrightarrow0.
\]

Twisting by \(P=L^{-1}\otimes N\) gives

\[
0\longrightarrow P
\longrightarrow Q^\vee\otimes N
\longrightarrow N\longrightarrow0. \tag{1}
\]

The middle term has rank two, degree three and determinant
\(P\otimes N=L^{-1}N^2\), exactly as the split pair \(P\oplus N\).
The previously proved stability of \(Q^\vee\) is preserved by tensoring, so
\(Q^\vee\otimes N\) is stable. Sequence (1) is consequently non-split:
\(P\oplus N\) would contain the degree-three destabilizing line \(N\).

Thus the stable replacement is not a new ansatz. It is the same
\(Q^\vee\otimes N\) already present on every generic support fiber.

## 4. Fourier-Mukai Repair

Every stable positive-degree bundle on an elliptic curve is WIT0. Therefore

\[
Q^\vee\otimes N:\quad (r,d)=(2,3)
\longmapsto(3,-2).
\]

The remaining line has

\[
L\otimes N:\quad(1,6)\longmapsto(6,-1).
\]

Their sum is the locally free twisted pair \((9,-3)\). Restoring the target
curving contributes \(+3\) to the degree and gives the ordinary determinant
row \((9,0)\).

The K-class identity

\[
[Q^\vee\otimes N]=[P]+[N]
\]

shows that the repair changes neither the special-fiber K-class nor its
determinant. It changes only the extension class, replacing the split,
mixed-WIT representative by its stable WIT0 representative.

## 5. Qutrit Covariance

The three syzygies form one exact theta-group orbit. Under the raising shift
\(S:T_j\mapsto T_{j+1}\), with the same action on the evaluation basis,

\[
s_0\mapsto s_1\mapsto s_2\mapsto s_0.
\]

Under the clock they have characters \(1,\omega^2,\omega\), respectively.
Thus the local stable reductions carry the dual qutrit representation and
are compatible with the already selected projective orbit at the fiber
tier. This is covariance of the local repair data, not yet a global Deligne
descent certificate.

